  "id": "seq-0017",
  "note": "R-74.4's look-back sufficiency proof, and the only fixture in the corpus written to make the bound BITE. maximumLookBackEvents is class C and owes evidence that 32 is sufficient rather than merely present; a corpus in which no window ever holds a thirty-third observation cannot supply it, because the truncation is then a no-op and the comparison proves nothing. Thirty-six activations inside a 175-second span put 36 observations in a 180-second window, so from step 32 onward the count bound really does discard the oldest - including the Pages activation the run opened with. The transcript is nevertheless byte-identical to an unbounded replay, which is the assertion SequenceReplayTests makes: the discarded observations could not have changed an answer, because the only questions the window is asked are whether there is a return anchor and how many distinct unrelated subjects there have been, and Pages held for five seconds - far short of returnAnchorDwell - so it was never an anchor to lose. The drift arithmetic is ordinary: grace from the first Steam activation, sustained at t=100 where the accumulator first passes ninety, one mandate, then cooldown for the rest.",
